\section*{Warm-up}
\textbf{1. Development time.} At 100 lines per day, Firefox (about 10 million lines) would take 100,000 days, or about 274 years. Google services (2,000 million lines) would take 20 million days, or about 54,795 years, assuming 365 working days per year.

\textbf{2. How large systems are built.} They are developed by teams over many years. Teams divide the work into modules, reuse existing components, and coordinate through design, code review, testing, and version control.

\textbf{3. Lines of code and productivity.} The 500-line solution is not automatically more productive than the 50-line solution. Correctness, clarity, maintainability, defects, delivery time, and user value are more useful measures than code volume alone.

\textbf{4. Human contribution with AI-generated code.} Developers still need to understand user needs, define requirements, choose designs, review results for correctness and safety, and take responsibility for the delivered system.

